---
import ReferenceTable from '../../components/ReferenceTable.astro';
import ContentCard from '../../components/ContentCard.astro';
import DocumentLayout from '../../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import { absoluteUrl, seo } from '../../data/seo';

const meta = seo.conformance;
const siteUrl = Astro.site?.toString() || 'https://tensor-standards.pages.dev/';
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  headline: meta.title,
  description: meta.description,
  url: absoluteUrl(siteUrl, meta.canonicalPath),
};
---

<DocumentLayout title={meta.title} description={meta.description} canonicalPath={meta.canonicalPath} ogType={meta.ogType} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <ContentCard>
<p class="section-kicker">Core 0.1.0-draft.1 · Reference validation</p>
    <h2>Validation scope</h2>
    <p>The reference validator checks structural and supported semantic requirements in the candidate JSON binding. It checks the supplied representation of an investigation; evidence truth, actual permissions, and operational effectiveness require separate evaluation.</p>
    <p><a href="/read/core/0.1.0-draft.1/coverage/">Detailed check coverage</a> · <a href="/implement/">Setup instructions</a> · <a href="/investigation-lab/">Browser validator</a></p>
    <h2>Reference commands</h2>
    <p>From the <a href="/review/">pinned Core review source</a>, with Node.js 24.21.0, npm 11.19.0, and locked dependencies installed:</p>
    <pre><code>{`npm run check:schemas
npm run validate:examples
npm test`}</code></pre>
    <h2>Result meanings</h2>
    <ReferenceTable label="Validation results"><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>pass</code></td><td>The supplied content satisfies the checks performed by this reference implementation.</td></tr><tr><td><code>fail</code></td><td>A checked structural or semantic rule is violated.</td></tr><tr><td><code>indeterminate</code></td><td>Required information or supported interpretation is insufficient to determine the affected meaning.</td></tr><tr><td><code>notRun</code></td><td>An individual check was not performed.</td></tr></tbody></ReferenceTable>
    <p>An investigative <code>unknown</code> outcome can be valid data. It is distinct from an indeterminate validation result.</p>
    <h2>Checked requirements</h2>
    <ul><li>Candidate structure, exact definition pins, and typed references.</li><li>Question subjects, time context, parameters, and explicit transition selections.</li><li>Revision lineage, original inputs, and required review representation.</li><li>Declared partial exchange, losses, and unsupported required dependencies.</li></ul>
    <h2>Implementation roles</h2>
    <p>Reports identify the roles implemented: definition producer, record producer, reader, lossless exchanger, or transition interpreter. Each role has a bounded claim and relevant checks in the <a href="/read/founding/01-core-contract/">Core contract</a>.</p>
    <p>Vendor and organization extensions also identify their namespace, version, required dependencies, and supported interpretation. The reference runtime retains optional opaque data and reports unsupported required meaning as indeterminate.</p>
    <h2>Implementation reports</h2>
    <p>A reproducible report includes specification and binding versions, implementation version, supported roles and profiles, artifact digests, test date, named checks, results, and exclusions. Exchange reports identify both implementations and any transformed, omitted, or unsupported meaning.</p>
    <p>The <a href="/read/founding/04-reference-cases-and-conformance/">twelve acceptance scenarios</a> describe the proposed evaluation scope. Independent interoperability requires exchange between separately developed implementations. No certification program is currently established.</p>
    <p><a href="/contribute/">Implementation report process</a> · <a href="/review/">Pinned review baseline</a> · <a href="/extensions/">Layer and extension formats</a></p>
  </ContentCard>
</DocumentLayout>
